Kent Paul Dolan wrote:

> APLer wrote:

>  > it's a game which lends itself fine to C alone.
 
> If that were the case the existing code would
> presumably be straightforward, well organized,
> modular, loosely coupled, easily maintainable, and
> easy to read. It is none of those things (and I've
> programmed in C since 1983, so I have the right to
> comment).

That isn't the fault of the language.  That's the 
fault of accumulated additions unsupported by the 
original design. 

Now it's true that there's a traditional style in 
C that makes it easy to limit your design.  It's 
true that C was developed before Object Oriented 
programming was an idea. It's also true that you 
can do things any way you want to, including object 
oriented, in C.  

>  > Why OO?>
 
> _Lots_ of NetHack is "standard behavior" plus
> "specialized behavior", monsters, grid cells,
> objects, and so on, and in several levels of
> increasing specialization. This cries out for the
> support for hierarchical code inheritance that OOs
> provide, to eliminate redundant code, and improve
> maintainablity and extensibility.

Yeah, one way or another you want "methods" as 
opposed to "functions".  Inheritance, meh, I don't 
care.  I think C++ is a particularly bad example 
of OO, actually, and I'm happier doing OO design 
in C because I want to use more advanced OO constructs 
than C++ implements and I don't want the stuff it 
does implement getting in my way.  But I know I'm 
not typical in that way. Most developers would 
rather not think that hard, and rolling your own 
OO framework specific to the project's needs is 
not something they want to deal with. 

C++ allows some limited OO stuff, it's familiar to 
a lot of people, and there are widespread compilers.  
As it settles into the "long tail" of obsolescence 
you'll see vendor-specific crap die off or converge 
and standards compliance become as important as it 
is for today's C compilers. For those reasons I'd 
say it's a good choice. 

